8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Today, we turn to the question of distributive justice.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
How should income and wealth and power

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,640
power and opportunities be distributed?

11
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:44,820
According to what principles?

12
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:49,340
John Rawls offers a detailed answer to that question.

13
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:51,440
And we're going to examine and assess his answer

14
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:53,720
to that question today.

15
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:55,720
We put ourselves in a

16
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:57,720
position to do so last time

17
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:02,760
by trying to make sense of why he thinks

18
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:06,920
that principles of justice are best derived

19
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,840
from a hypothetical contract.

20
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:19,440
And what matters is that the hypothetical contract

21
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:23,600
be carried out in an original position of equality

22
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:27,600
behind what Rawls calls the veil of ignorance.

23
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:31,259
So that much is clear.

24
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:36,760
All right, then let's turn to the principles

25
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:41,720
that Rawls says would be

26
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:43,800
chosen behind the veil of ignorance.

27
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:55,880
First he considers some of the major alternatives.

28
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:58,780
What about utilitarianism?

29
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:02,500
Would the people in the original position choose to govern,

30
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:07,000
their collective lives, utilitarian principles,

31
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,639
the greatest good for the greatest number?

32
00:02:09,639 --> 00:02:13,880
No, they wouldn't, Rawls says.

33
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:18,680
And the reason is that behind the veil of ignorance,

34
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:23,680
everyone knows that once the veil goes up

35
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:27,680
and real life begins, we

36
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:31,680
will each want to be respected

37
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:32,680
with dignity.

38
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:35,680
Even if we turn out to

39
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:38,680
be a member of a minority,

40
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:42,680
we don't want to be oppressed.

41
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:47,680
And so we would agree to reject utilitarianism

42
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:53,680
and instead to adopt as our first principle

43
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:56,680
equal basic liberties, fundamental

44
00:02:56,680 --> 00:02:59,680
rights to freedom of speech,

45
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:00,680
freedom of assembly, and freedom of the people.

46
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:03,680
A new world, a new society, religious liberty,

47
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:05,680
freedom of conscience, and the like.

48
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:08,680
We wouldn't want to take the

49
00:03:08,680 --> 00:03:11,680
chance that we would wind up

50
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:19,680
as members of an oppressed or a despised minority

51
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:23,680
with the majority tyrannizing over us.

52
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:27,680
And so Rawls says utilitarianism would be rejected.

53
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:36,320
Utilitarianism makes the mistake, Rawls writes, of

54
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:40,960
forgetting, or at least not taking seriously,

55
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:44,720
the distinction between persons.

56
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:46,790
And in the original position behind the

57
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:48,860
veil of ignorance, we would recognize that and

58
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:52,380
reject utilitarianism.

59
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:55,560
We wouldn't trade off our fundamental

60
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:58,740
rights and liberties for any economic advantages.

61
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:03,580
That's the first principle.

62
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:06,270
Second principle has to do

63
00:04:06,270 --> 00:04:08,960
with social and economic inequalities.

64
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:10,860
What would we agree to?

65
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:13,870
Remember, we don't know whether we're going to

66
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:16,880
wind up being rich or poor, healthy or

67
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:18,740
unhealthy.

68
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:21,100
We don't know what kind of family we're

69
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:23,460
going to come from, whether we're going to inherit

70
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:26,000
millions or whether we will

71
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,540
come from an impoverished family.

72
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:28,640
Okay?

73
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:34,960
So we might, at first thought, say,

74
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:41,180
well, let's require an equal distribution of income

75
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:47,340
and wealth, just to be on the safe side.

76
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:52,240
But then we would realize that we could

77
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:57,140
do better than that, even if we're unlucky and

78
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:58,540
wind up at the bottom.

79
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:02,890
We could do better if we

80
00:05:02,890 --> 00:05:07,040
agree to a qualified principle of equality.

81
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:10,510
Rawls calls it the difference principle, a

82
00:05:10,510 --> 00:05:13,980
principle that says only those social and

83
00:05:13,980 --> 00:05:18,260
economic inequalities will be permitted that work

84
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:22,540
to the benefit of the least well-off.

85
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:28,220
So we wouldn't reject all inequality of income and wealth.

86
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:31,910
We would allow some, but the test would be,

87
00:05:31,910 --> 00:05:35,600
do they work to the benefit of everyone, including

88
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:40,660
those, or as he specifies the

89
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:45,720
principle, especially those at the bottom.

90
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:47,880
Only those inequalities would be accepted behind

91
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:50,040
the veil of ignorance, and so Rawls

92
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:54,030
argues, only those inequalities that work to

93
00:05:54,030 --> 00:05:58,020
the benefit of the least well-off are just.

94
00:05:58,020 --> 00:06:02,460
We talked about the examples of Michael Jordan

95
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:06,900
making $31 million a year, of Bill Gates having

96
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:11,880
a fortune in the tens of billions.

97
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:14,330
Would those inequalities be

98
00:06:14,330 --> 00:06:16,780
permitted under the difference principle?

99
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:20,200
Only if they were part of a

100
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:23,620
system, those wage differentials, that actually work to

101
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:25,520
the advantage of the least well-off.

102
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:27,820
Well, what would that system be?

103
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:57,060
Only if they were part of theigs part of the

104
00:06:57,060 --> 00:06:57,580
difference principle, for example.

105
00:06:57,580 --> 00:07:03,140
Let me hear what you think about Rawls' claim that

106
00:07:03,140 --> 00:07:08,700
these two principles would be chosen behind the veil of ignorance.

107
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:13,500
Is there anyone who disagrees that they would be chosen?

108
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:16,350
All right, let's start up in the

109
00:07:16,350 --> 00:07:17,700
balcony if that's all right. Go ahead.

110
00:07:18,039 --> 00:07:21,479
Okay, your argument depends upon us believing that we

111
00:07:21,479 --> 00:07:24,919
would argue and set policy or justice from a bottom,

112
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:27,390
for the disadvantaged, and I just don't see

113
00:07:27,390 --> 00:07:29,860
from a proof standpoint where we've proven that.

114
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:30,740
Why not the top?

115
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:32,620
Right, and what's your name?

116
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:33,200
Mike.

117
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:35,020
Mike, all right, good question.

118
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:39,220
Put yourself behind the veil of ignorance.

119
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,400
Enter into the thought experiment.

120
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:45,180
What principles would you choose?

121
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:46,880
How would you think it through?

122
00:07:47,940 --> 00:07:50,210
Well, I would say things like even Harvard's

123
00:07:50,210 --> 00:07:52,480
existence is an example of preaching toward the top

124
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:54,580
because Harvard takes the top academics.

125
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:55,580
And I didn't know when I

126
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:56,580
was born how smart I would be,

127
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:58,330
but I worked my life to

128
00:07:58,330 --> 00:08:00,080
get to a place of this caliber.

129
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:02,450
Now, if you'd said Harvard's going to

130
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:04,820
randomly take 1,600 people of absolutely no qualification,

131
00:08:04,820 --> 00:08:06,320
we'd all be saying, well,

132
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:07,820
there's not much to work for.

133
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:11,820
And so what principle would you choose?

134
00:08:12,820 --> 00:08:14,820
In that situation, I would say a merit-based one,

135
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:16,820
one where I don't necessarily know what I have,

136
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:18,820
but I'd rather have a system

137
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:20,820
that rewards me based on my efforts.

138
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:23,820
So you, Mike, behind the veil of ignorance,

139
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:27,580
would choose a merit-based system where

140
00:08:27,580 --> 00:08:30,580
people are rewarded according to their efforts.

141
00:08:30,580 --> 00:08:32,579
All right, fair enough.

142
00:08:32,579 --> 00:08:34,579
What would you say? Go ahead.

143
00:08:34,579 --> 00:08:38,579
My question is if the merit-based argument is based

144
00:08:38,579 --> 00:08:42,579
on when everyone is at a level of equality,

145
00:08:42,579 --> 00:08:49,579
where from that position you're rewarded to where you get,

146
00:08:49,579 --> 00:08:54,579
or is it regardless of what advantages you may have?

147
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:56,580
When you began your education

148
00:08:56,580 --> 00:08:58,580
to get where you are here?

149
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:01,580
I think the question you're asking is saying,

150
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:03,080
if you want to look

151
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:04,580
at whatever utilitarianism policy it is,

152
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:06,580
do we want to maximize world wealth?

153
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:09,080
And I think a system that rewards merit

154
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:11,580
is the one that we've pretty much all established

155
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:13,580
is what is best for all of us.

156
00:09:13,580 --> 00:09:15,080
Despite the fact that some of

157
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:16,580
us may be in the second percentile

158
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:18,580
and some may be in the 98th percentile,

159
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:20,580
at the end of the day

160
00:09:20,580 --> 00:09:22,580
it lifts that lowest base level,

161
00:09:22,580 --> 00:09:24,580
a community that rewards merit.

162
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:27,580
And that's effort as opposed to innate differences.

163
00:09:27,580 --> 00:09:30,080
But I don't understand

164
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:32,580
how you're rewarding someone's efforts

165
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:36,580
who clearly has had, not you but maybe myself,

166
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:39,580
advantages throughout to get where I am here.

167
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:41,580
I mean, I can't say that somebody else

168
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:43,580
who maybe worked as hard as I did

169
00:09:43,580 --> 00:09:45,580
would have had the same opportunity

170
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:47,580
to come to a school like this.

171
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:50,580
All right, let's look at that point. What's your name?

172
00:09:50,580 --> 00:09:51,580
Kate.

173
00:09:51,580 --> 00:09:53,580
Kate, you suspect that the,

174
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:58,580
that the ability to get into top schools

175
00:09:58,580 --> 00:10:05,580
may largely depend on coming from an affluent family,

176
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:09,580
having a favorable back, family background,

177
00:10:09,580 --> 00:10:12,580
social, cultural, economic advantages and so on?

178
00:10:12,580 --> 00:10:14,580
I mean economic, but yes, social, cultural,

179
00:10:14,580 --> 00:10:16,580
all of those advantages for sure.

180
00:10:16,580 --> 00:10:19,580
Someone did a study of

181
00:10:19,580 --> 00:10:22,580
the 146 selective colleges and universities

182
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:25,580
in the United States.

183
00:10:25,580 --> 00:10:28,080
And they looked at the

184
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:30,580
students in those colleges and universities

185
00:10:30,580 --> 00:10:34,580
to try to find out what their background was,

186
00:10:34,580 --> 00:10:37,580
their economic background.

187
00:10:37,580 --> 00:10:41,580
What percentage do you think come from the bottom quarter

188
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:44,580
of the income scale?

189
00:10:44,580 --> 00:10:46,580
You know what the figure is?

190
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:49,080
Only 3% of students at

191
00:10:49,080 --> 00:10:51,580
the most selective colleges and universities

192
00:10:51,580 --> 00:10:54,580
come from poor backgrounds.

193
00:10:54,580 --> 00:11:00,580
Over 70% come from affluent families.

194
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:03,080
Let's go one step further then

195
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:05,580
and try to address Mike's challenge.

196
00:11:05,580 --> 00:11:11,580
Rawls actually has two arguments, not one,

197
00:11:11,580 --> 00:11:15,580
in favor of his principles of justice,

198
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:18,580
and in particular of the difference principle.

199
00:11:18,580 --> 00:11:21,580
One argument is the official argument.

200
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:24,580
What would be chosen behind the veil of ignorance?

201
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:28,580
Some people challenge that argument saying,

202
00:11:28,580 --> 00:11:33,580
maybe people would want to take their chances.

203
00:11:33,580 --> 00:11:37,580
Maybe people would be gamblers behind the veil of ignorance,

204
00:11:37,580 --> 00:11:41,580
hoping that they would wind up on top.

205
00:11:41,580 --> 00:11:44,580
That's one challenge that has been put to Rawls.

206
00:11:44,580 --> 00:11:48,580
But backing up the argument from the original position

207
00:11:48,580 --> 00:11:50,580
is a second argument.

208
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:54,580
And that is a straightforwardly moral argument.

209
00:11:54,580 --> 00:11:57,580
And it goes like this.

210
00:11:57,580 --> 00:11:59,580
It says,

211
00:11:59,580 --> 00:12:03,580
the distribution of income and wealth and opportunities

212
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:09,580
should not be based on factors

213
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:13,580
for which people can claim no credit.

214
00:12:13,580 --> 00:12:16,580
It shouldn't be based on factors that are arbitrary

215
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:19,580
from a moral point of view.

216
00:12:19,580 --> 00:12:23,580
Rawls illustrates this by considering

217
00:12:23,580 --> 00:12:28,580
several rival theories of justice.

218
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:34,580
He begins with the theory of justice

219
00:12:34,580 --> 00:12:37,580
that most everyone these days would reject.

220
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:41,580
A feudal aristocracy.

221
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:46,580
What's wrong with the allocation of life prospects

222
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:48,580
in a feudal aristocracy?

223
00:12:49,580 --> 00:12:50,580
Rawls says,

224
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:52,580
well, the thing that's obviously wrong about it

225
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:56,580
is that people's life prospects are determined

226
00:12:56,580 --> 00:12:59,580
by the accident of birth.

227
00:12:59,580 --> 00:13:00,580
Are you born to a noble family

228
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:03,580
or to the family of peasants and serfs?

229
00:13:03,580 --> 00:13:04,580
And that's it.

230
00:13:04,580 --> 00:13:05,580
You can't rise.

231
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:07,580
It's not your doing where you wind up

232
00:13:07,580 --> 00:13:11,580
or what opportunities you have.

233
00:13:11,580 --> 00:13:15,580
But that's arbitrary from a moral point of view.

234
00:13:15,580 --> 00:13:18,580
And so that objection to feudal aristocracy,

235
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:25,580
leads and historically has led people to say,

236
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:28,580
careers should be open to talents.

237
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:31,580
There should be formal equality of opportunity

238
00:13:31,580 --> 00:13:34,580
regardless of the accident of birth.

239
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:37,580
Every person should be free to strive,

240
00:13:37,580 --> 00:13:42,580
to work, to apply for any job in the society.

241
00:13:42,580 --> 00:13:45,580
And then, if you open up jobs

242
00:13:45,580 --> 00:13:47,580
and you allow people to apply,

243
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:49,580
and to work as hard as they can,

244
00:13:49,580 --> 00:13:53,580
then the results are just.

245
00:13:53,580 --> 00:13:56,580
So it's more or less the libertarian system

246
00:13:56,580 --> 00:13:58,580
that we've discussed in earlier weeks.

247
00:13:58,580 --> 00:14:00,580
What does Rawls think about this?

248
00:14:00,580 --> 00:14:02,580
He says it's an improvement.

249
00:14:02,580 --> 00:14:06,580
It's an improvement because it doesn't take as fixed

250
00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:09,580
the accident of birth.

251
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:12,580
But even with formal equality of opportunity,

252
00:14:12,580 --> 00:14:16,580
the libertarian conception doesn't extend that

253
00:14:16,580 --> 00:14:20,580
doesn't extend its insight far enough.

254
00:14:20,580 --> 00:14:24,580
Because if you let everybody run the race,

255
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:27,580
everybody can enter the race,

256
00:14:27,580 --> 00:14:31,580
but some people start at different starting points,

257
00:14:31,580 --> 00:14:34,580
that race isn't going to be fair.

258
00:14:34,580 --> 00:14:35,580
Intuitively, he says,

259
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:38,580
the most obvious injustice of this system

260
00:14:38,580 --> 00:14:40,580
is that it permits distributive shares

261
00:14:40,580 --> 00:14:42,580
to be improperly influenced by factors

262
00:14:42,580 --> 00:14:45,580
arbitrary from a moral point of view,

263
00:14:45,580 --> 00:14:48,580
such as whether you got a good education or not,

264
00:14:48,580 --> 00:14:51,580
whether you grew up in a family that supported you

265
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:53,580
and developed in you a work ethic

266
00:14:53,580 --> 00:14:56,580
and gave you the opportunities.

267
00:14:56,580 --> 00:15:01,580
So that suggests moving to a system of fair

268
00:15:01,580 --> 00:15:03,580
equality of opportunity,

269
00:15:03,580 --> 00:15:06,580
and that's really the system that Mike was advocating

270
00:15:06,580 --> 00:15:07,580
earlier on.

271
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:10,580
What we might call a merit-based system,

272
00:15:10,580 --> 00:15:12,580
a meritocratic system.

273
00:15:12,580 --> 00:15:14,580
In a fair meritocracy,

274
00:15:14,580 --> 00:15:17,580
the society sets up institutions

275
00:15:17,580 --> 00:15:20,580
to bring everyone to the same starting point

276
00:15:20,580 --> 00:15:23,580
before the race begins.

277
00:15:23,580 --> 00:15:26,580
Equal educational opportunities,

278
00:15:26,580 --> 00:15:30,580
Head Start programs, for example,

279
00:15:30,580 --> 00:15:35,580
support for schools in impoverished neighborhoods,

280
00:15:35,580 --> 00:15:39,580
so that everyone, regardless of their family background,

281
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:43,580
has a genuinely fair opportunity,

282
00:15:43,580 --> 00:15:47,580
everyone starts from the same starting line.

283
00:15:47,580 --> 00:15:50,580
Well, what does Rawls think about

284
00:15:50,580 --> 00:15:52,580
the meritocratic system?

285
00:15:55,580 --> 00:15:57,580
Even that, he says,

286
00:15:57,580 --> 00:16:00,580
doesn't go far enough in remedying

287
00:16:00,580 --> 00:16:02,580
or addressing

288
00:16:02,580 --> 00:16:05,580
the moral arbitrariness

289
00:16:07,580 --> 00:16:09,580
of the natural lottery.

290
00:16:10,580 --> 00:16:11,580
Because if you bring everyone

291
00:16:11,580 --> 00:16:13,580
to the same starting point,

292
00:16:13,580 --> 00:16:17,580
and begin the race,

293
00:16:17,580 --> 00:16:20,580
who's going to win the race?

294
00:16:20,580 --> 00:16:22,580
Who would win?

295
00:16:22,580 --> 00:16:25,580
To use the runner's example.

296
00:16:25,580 --> 00:16:28,580
The fastest runners would win.

297
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:31,580
But is it their doing

298
00:16:31,580 --> 00:16:33,580
that they happen to be blessed

299
00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:36,580
with the athletic prowess to run fast?

300
00:16:41,580 --> 00:16:42,580
So Rawls says,

301
00:16:42,580 --> 00:16:46,580
even the principle of meritocracy,

302
00:16:46,580 --> 00:16:49,580
where you bring everyone to the same starting point,

303
00:16:49,580 --> 00:16:52,580
may eliminate the influence of social contingencies

304
00:16:52,580 --> 00:16:53,580
and upbringing,

305
00:16:53,580 --> 00:16:55,580
but it still permits the distribution

306
00:16:55,580 --> 00:16:57,580
of wealth and income to be determined

307
00:16:57,580 --> 00:16:59,580
by the natural distribution

308
00:16:59,580 --> 00:17:02,580
of abilities and talents.

309
00:17:02,580 --> 00:17:04,579
And so he thinks

310
00:17:04,579 --> 00:17:05,579
that the principle of eliminating

311
00:17:05,579 --> 00:17:07,579
morally arbitrary influences

312
00:17:07,579 --> 00:17:09,579
in the distribution of income and wealth

313
00:17:09,579 --> 00:17:11,579
requires going beyond

314
00:17:11,579 --> 00:17:14,579
what Mike favors,

315
00:17:14,579 --> 00:17:17,579
the meritocratic system.

316
00:17:17,579 --> 00:17:20,579
Now, how do you go beyond?

317
00:17:20,579 --> 00:17:23,579
If you bring everyone to the same starting point,

318
00:17:23,579 --> 00:17:25,579
and you're still bothered by the fact

319
00:17:25,579 --> 00:17:26,579
that some are fast runners

320
00:17:26,579 --> 00:17:28,580
and some are not fast runners,

321
00:17:28,580 --> 00:17:30,580
what can you do?

322
00:17:30,580 --> 00:17:32,580
Well, some critics

323
00:17:32,580 --> 00:17:35,580
of a more egalitarian conception

324
00:17:35,580 --> 00:17:37,580
say the only thing you can do

325
00:17:37,580 --> 00:17:40,580
is handicap the fast runners.

326
00:17:40,580 --> 00:17:42,580
Make them wear lead shoes.

327
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:44,580
But who wants to do that?

328
00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:47,580
That would defeat the whole point

329
00:17:47,580 --> 00:17:49,580
of running the race.

330
00:17:50,580 --> 00:17:52,580
But Rawls says,

331
00:17:52,580 --> 00:17:53,580
you don't have to have

332
00:17:53,580 --> 00:17:55,580
a kind of leveling equality

333
00:17:55,580 --> 00:17:57,580
if you want to go beyond

334
00:17:57,580 --> 00:17:59,580
a meritocratic conception.

335
00:17:59,580 --> 00:18:01,580
You permit,

336
00:18:01,580 --> 00:18:03,580
you even encourage,

337
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:06,580
those who may be gifted

338
00:18:06,580 --> 00:18:08,580
to exercise their talents.

339
00:18:08,580 --> 00:18:10,580
But what you do,

340
00:18:10,580 --> 00:18:12,580
is you change the terms

341
00:18:12,580 --> 00:18:14,580
on which people are entitled

342
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:16,580
to the fruits of the exercise

343
00:18:16,580 --> 00:18:18,580
of those talents.

344
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:19,580
And that really is what

345
00:18:19,580 --> 00:18:21,580
the difference principle is.

346
00:18:21,580 --> 00:18:24,580
You establish a principle that says,

347
00:18:24,580 --> 00:18:25,580
people may benefit

348
00:18:25,580 --> 00:18:28,580
from their good fortune,

349
00:18:28,580 --> 00:18:31,580
from their luck in the genetic lottery,

350
00:18:31,580 --> 00:18:33,580
but only on terms

351
00:18:33,580 --> 00:18:34,580
that work to the advantage

352
00:18:34,580 --> 00:18:35,580
of the least well-off.

353
00:18:35,580 --> 00:18:38,580
And so, for example,

354
00:18:40,580 --> 00:18:42,580
Michael Jordan can make

355
00:18:42,580 --> 00:18:43,580
$31 million,

356
00:18:43,580 --> 00:18:46,580
but only under a system

357
00:18:46,580 --> 00:18:48,580
that taxes away

358
00:18:48,580 --> 00:18:49,580
a chunk of that

359
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:51,580
to help those who lack

360
00:18:51,580 --> 00:18:52,580
the basketball skills

361
00:18:52,580 --> 00:18:54,580
that he's blessed with.

362
00:18:55,580 --> 00:18:57,580
Likewise, Bill Gates,

363
00:18:57,580 --> 00:18:59,580
he can make his billions,

364
00:18:59,580 --> 00:19:01,580
but he can't think that he somehow

365
00:19:01,580 --> 00:19:04,580
morally deserves those billions.

366
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:07,580
Those who have been favored by nature

367
00:19:08,580 --> 00:19:10,580
may gain from their good fortune,

368
00:19:10,580 --> 00:19:11,580
but only on terms

369
00:19:11,580 --> 00:19:12,580
that improve the situation

370
00:19:12,580 --> 00:19:14,580
of those who have lost out.

371
00:19:14,580 --> 00:19:16,580
That's the difference principle.

372
00:19:16,580 --> 00:19:17,580
And it's an argument

373
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:19,580
from moral arbitrariness.

374
00:19:19,580 --> 00:19:21,580
Rawls claims that if you're bothered

375
00:19:21,580 --> 00:19:23,580
by basing distributive shares

376
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:24,580
on factors arbitrary

377
00:19:24,580 --> 00:19:26,580
from a moral point of view,

378
00:19:26,580 --> 00:19:27,580
you don't just reject

379
00:19:27,580 --> 00:19:28,580
a feudal aristocracy

380
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:31,580
for a free market.

381
00:19:31,580 --> 00:19:35,580
You don't even rest content

382
00:19:35,580 --> 00:19:37,580
with a meritocratic system

383
00:19:37,580 --> 00:19:38,580
that brings everyone

384
00:19:38,580 --> 00:19:40,580
to the same starting point.

385
00:19:40,580 --> 00:19:42,580
You set up a system

386
00:19:42,580 --> 00:19:43,580
where everyone,

387
00:19:43,580 --> 00:19:45,580
including those at the bottom,

388
00:19:45,580 --> 00:19:46,580
benefit from the exercise

389
00:19:46,580 --> 00:19:47,580
of the talents

390
00:19:47,580 --> 00:19:48,580
held by those

391
00:19:48,580 --> 00:19:51,580
who happen to be lucky.

392
00:19:51,580 --> 00:19:53,580
What do you think?

393
00:19:53,580 --> 00:19:55,580
Is that persuasive?

394
00:19:57,580 --> 00:19:59,580
Who finds that argument

395
00:19:59,580 --> 00:20:00,580
unpersuasive,

396
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:01,580
the argument

397
00:20:01,580 --> 00:20:03,580
from moral arbitrariness?

398
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:05,580
Yes?

399
00:20:05,580 --> 00:20:08,580
I think that in the egalitarian

400
00:20:08,580 --> 00:20:09,580
proposition,

401
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:11,580
the more talented people,

402
00:20:11,580 --> 00:20:12,580
I think it's very optimistic

403
00:20:12,580 --> 00:20:14,580
to think that they would still

404
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:16,580
work really hard

405
00:20:16,580 --> 00:20:17,580
even if they knew

406
00:20:17,580 --> 00:20:19,580
that part of what they made

407
00:20:19,580 --> 00:20:20,580
would be given away.

408
00:20:20,580 --> 00:20:22,580
So I think that the only way

409
00:20:22,580 --> 00:20:24,580
for the more talented people

410
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:25,580
to exercise their talents

411
00:20:25,580 --> 00:20:27,580
to the best of their ability

412
00:20:27,580 --> 00:20:29,580
is in the meritocracy.

413
00:20:29,580 --> 00:20:30,580
And in a meritocracy,

414
00:20:30,580 --> 00:20:31,580
what's your name?

415
00:20:31,580 --> 00:20:32,580
Kate.

416
00:20:32,580 --> 00:20:33,580
Kate.

417
00:20:33,580 --> 00:20:34,580
Does it bother you?

418
00:20:34,580 --> 00:20:37,580
And Mike, does it bother you?

419
00:20:37,580 --> 00:20:38,580
I think that the more talented

420
00:20:38,580 --> 00:20:39,580
people,

421
00:20:39,580 --> 00:20:40,580
the more talented they are,

422
00:20:40,580 --> 00:20:41,580
the more talented they are

423
00:20:41,580 --> 00:20:42,580
in the meritocracy.

424
00:20:42,580 --> 00:20:43,580
And I think that

425
00:20:43,580 --> 00:20:44,580
in a meritocratic system,

426
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:45,580
even with fair equality

427
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:47,580
of opportunity,

428
00:20:47,580 --> 00:20:48,580
people get ahead,

429
00:20:48,580 --> 00:20:49,580
people get rewards

430
00:20:49,580 --> 00:20:51,580
that they don't deserve

431
00:20:51,580 --> 00:20:52,580
simply because they happen

432
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:54,580
to be naturally gifted.

433
00:20:54,580 --> 00:20:56,580
What about that?

434
00:20:56,580 --> 00:20:59,580
I think that it is arbitrary

435
00:20:59,580 --> 00:21:01,580
and obviously it's arbitrary,

436
00:21:01,580 --> 00:21:03,580
but I think that they're,

437
00:21:03,580 --> 00:21:04,580
that correcting for it

438
00:21:04,580 --> 00:21:06,580
would be detrimental

439
00:21:06,580 --> 00:21:07,580
and unlike...

440
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:08,580
Because it would reduce

441
00:21:08,580 --> 00:21:09,580
the value of the race.

442
00:21:09,580 --> 00:21:10,580
Yeah.

443
00:21:10,580 --> 00:21:11,580
Mike, what do you say?

444
00:21:11,580 --> 00:21:12,580
We're all sitting in this room

445
00:21:12,580 --> 00:21:14,580
and we have undeserved,

446
00:21:14,580 --> 00:21:15,580
we have undeserved glory

447
00:21:15,580 --> 00:21:16,580
of some sort

448
00:21:16,580 --> 00:21:17,580
so you should not be satisfied

449
00:21:17,580 --> 00:21:19,580
with the process of your life

450
00:21:19,580 --> 00:21:20,580
because you have not created

451
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:21,580
any of this.

452
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:22,580
And I think from a standpoint

453
00:21:22,580 --> 00:21:23,580
of not just this room

454
00:21:23,580 --> 00:21:24,580
us being upset,

455
00:21:24,580 --> 00:21:26,580
but from a societal standpoint,

456
00:21:26,580 --> 00:21:27,580
we should have some kind of

457
00:21:27,580 --> 00:21:29,580
a gut reaction to that feeling

458
00:21:29,580 --> 00:21:30,580
that, you know,

459
00:21:30,580 --> 00:21:31,580
the guy who runs the race,

460
00:21:31,580 --> 00:21:32,580
he doesn't,

461
00:21:32,580 --> 00:21:33,580
he actually harms us

462
00:21:33,580 --> 00:21:34,580
as opposed to maybe makes me

463
00:21:34,580 --> 00:21:35,580
run that last 10 yards faster

464
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:37,580
and that makes the guy behind me

465
00:21:37,580 --> 00:21:38,580
run 10 yards faster

466
00:21:38,580 --> 00:21:39,580
and the guy behind him

467
00:21:39,580 --> 00:21:40,580
10 yards faster.

468
00:21:40,580 --> 00:21:41,580
All right, so Mike,

469
00:21:41,580 --> 00:21:42,580
let me ask you,

470
00:21:42,580 --> 00:21:43,580
you talked about effort before.

471
00:21:43,580 --> 00:21:44,580
Effort.

472
00:21:44,580 --> 00:21:47,580
Do you think when people work hard

473
00:21:47,580 --> 00:21:49,580
to get ahead and succeed

474
00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:52,580
that they deserve the rewards

475
00:21:52,580 --> 00:21:54,580
that go with effort?

476
00:21:54,580 --> 00:21:55,580
Isn't that the idea

477
00:21:55,580 --> 00:21:56,580
behind your defense of...

478
00:21:56,580 --> 00:21:57,580
I mean, of course,

479
00:21:57,580 --> 00:21:58,580
bring Michael Jordan here,

480
00:21:58,580 --> 00:21:59,580
I'm sure you can get him,

481
00:21:59,580 --> 00:22:00,580
and have him come

482
00:22:00,580 --> 00:22:01,580
and defend himself

483
00:22:01,580 --> 00:22:02,580
about why he makes $31 million

484
00:22:02,580 --> 00:22:03,580
and I think what you're going

485
00:22:03,580 --> 00:22:04,580
to realize is his life

486
00:22:04,580 --> 00:22:05,580
was a very, very tough one

487
00:22:05,580 --> 00:22:06,580
to get to the top

488
00:22:06,580 --> 00:22:07,580
and that we are very,

489
00:22:07,580 --> 00:22:08,580
very proud of him

490
00:22:08,580 --> 00:22:09,580
for basically being

491
00:22:09,580 --> 00:22:10,580
the majority oppressing

492
00:22:10,580 --> 00:22:11,580
the minority in a different light.

493
00:22:11,580 --> 00:22:12,580
It's very easy to pick on him.

494
00:22:12,580 --> 00:22:13,580
All right.

495
00:22:13,580 --> 00:22:14,580
Very easy.

496
00:22:14,580 --> 00:22:15,580
Effort.

497
00:22:15,580 --> 00:22:16,580
You know what?

498
00:22:17,580 --> 00:22:18,580
All right, you've got...

499
00:22:18,580 --> 00:22:19,580
I've got a few.

500
00:22:19,580 --> 00:22:20,580
You've persuaded a lot of people.

501
00:22:20,580 --> 00:22:21,580
I've got a few,

502
00:22:21,580 --> 00:22:22,580
but that's about it.

503
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:23,580
Effort.

504
00:22:23,580 --> 00:22:24,580
You know what

505
00:22:24,580 --> 00:22:27,580
Raul's answer to that is?

506
00:22:27,580 --> 00:22:29,580
Even the effort

507
00:22:29,580 --> 00:22:32,580
that some people expend,

508
00:22:32,580 --> 00:22:33,580
conscientious driving,

509
00:22:33,580 --> 00:22:35,580
the work ethic,

510
00:22:35,580 --> 00:22:37,580
even effort depends a lot

511
00:22:37,580 --> 00:22:38,580
on fortunate results

512
00:22:38,580 --> 00:22:40,580
and family circumstances

513
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:41,580
for which you,

514
00:22:41,580 --> 00:22:42,580
we,

515
00:22:42,580 --> 00:22:44,580
can claim no credit.

516
00:22:44,580 --> 00:22:46,580
Now, let's...

517
00:22:46,580 --> 00:22:48,580
Wait, we're going to let...

518
00:22:48,580 --> 00:22:50,580
Let's do the test.

519
00:22:50,580 --> 00:22:52,580
Let's do a test here.

520
00:22:52,580 --> 00:22:54,580
Never mind economic class.

521
00:22:54,580 --> 00:22:55,580
Those differences

522
00:22:55,580 --> 00:22:56,580
are very significant.

523
00:22:56,580 --> 00:22:58,580
Put those aside.

524
00:22:58,580 --> 00:23:01,580
Psychologists say that birth order

525
00:23:01,580 --> 00:23:03,580
makes a lot of difference

526
00:23:03,580 --> 00:23:04,580
in work ethic,

527
00:23:04,580 --> 00:23:07,580
striving, effort.

528
00:23:07,580 --> 00:23:08,580
How many here...

529
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:09,580
Raise your hand,

530
00:23:09,580 --> 00:23:10,580
those of you here

531
00:23:10,580 --> 00:23:13,580
who are first in birth order.

532
00:23:17,580 --> 00:23:19,580
I am too, by the way.

533
00:23:22,580 --> 00:23:24,580
Mike, I noticed you raised your hand.

534
00:23:27,580 --> 00:23:29,580
If the case

535
00:23:29,580 --> 00:23:32,580
for the meritocratic conception

536
00:23:32,580 --> 00:23:35,580
is that effort should be rewarded,

537
00:23:35,580 --> 00:23:36,580
doesn't Raul's answer

538
00:23:36,580 --> 00:23:38,580
have a point

539
00:23:38,580 --> 00:23:40,580
that even effort,

540
00:23:40,580 --> 00:23:41,580
striving,

541
00:23:41,580 --> 00:23:42,580
work ethic

542
00:23:42,580 --> 00:23:44,580
is largely shaped

543
00:23:44,580 --> 00:23:47,580
even by birth order?

544
00:23:47,580 --> 00:23:49,580
Is it your doing?

545
00:23:49,580 --> 00:23:50,580
Mike, is it your doing

546
00:23:50,580 --> 00:23:53,580
that you were first in birth order?

547
00:23:53,580 --> 00:23:56,580
Then why, Raul says,

548
00:23:56,580 --> 00:23:58,580
of course not.

549
00:23:58,580 --> 00:24:01,580
So why should income and wealth

550
00:24:01,580 --> 00:24:04,580
and opportunities in life

551
00:24:04,580 --> 00:24:05,580
be based on birth order?

552
00:24:05,580 --> 00:24:07,580
Why should the birth order

553
00:24:07,580 --> 00:24:09,580
be based on factors

554
00:24:09,580 --> 00:24:11,580
arbitrary from a moral point of view?

555
00:24:11,580 --> 00:24:14,580
That's a challenge that he puts

556
00:24:14,580 --> 00:24:18,580
to market societies,

557
00:24:18,580 --> 00:24:21,580
but also

558
00:24:21,580 --> 00:24:23,580
to those of us

559
00:24:23,580 --> 00:24:25,580
at places like this.

560
00:24:25,580 --> 00:24:26,580
A question to think about

561
00:24:26,580 --> 00:24:27,580
for next time.

562
00:24:35,580 --> 00:24:38,580
A justice of the United States Supreme Court.

563
00:24:38,580 --> 00:24:41,580
What do they make?

564
00:24:41,580 --> 00:24:45,580
It's just under $200,000.

565
00:24:45,580 --> 00:24:47,580
But there's another judge

566
00:24:47,580 --> 00:24:48,580
who makes a lot more

567
00:24:48,580 --> 00:24:50,580
than Sandra Day O'Connor.

568
00:24:52,580 --> 00:24:53,580
Do you know who it is?

569
00:24:53,580 --> 00:24:54,580
Judge Judy.

570
00:24:54,580 --> 00:24:55,580
Judge Judy.

571
00:24:55,580 --> 00:24:56,580
How did you know that?

572
