Get over it, and let's see if we can

854
00:38:12,380 --> 00:38:15,380
at least maximize the benefits that flow from it.

855
00:38:17,380 --> 00:38:20,380
But the more powerful libertarian objection to Rawls

856
00:38:20,380 --> 00:38:23,380
is not from the libertarian economists

857
00:38:23,380 --> 00:38:25,380
like Milton Friedman.

858
00:38:25,380 --> 00:38:29,380
It's from the argument about self-ownership,

859
00:38:29,380 --> 00:38:32,380
developed, as we saw, in Nozick.

860
00:38:35,380 --> 00:38:37,380
And from that point of view,

861
00:38:37,380 --> 00:38:40,380
yes, it might be a good thing

862
00:38:40,380 --> 00:38:42,380
to create Head Start programs

863
00:38:42,380 --> 00:38:44,380
and public schools

864
00:38:44,380 --> 00:38:46,380
so that everyone can go to a decent school

865
00:38:46,380 --> 00:38:49,380
and start the race at the same starting line.

866
00:38:49,380 --> 00:38:51,380
That might be good.

867
00:38:51,380 --> 00:38:55,380
But if you tax people to create public schools,

868
00:38:55,380 --> 00:38:57,380
if you tax people

869
00:38:57,380 --> 00:38:59,380
against their will,

870
00:38:59,380 --> 00:39:02,380
you coerce them.

871
00:39:02,380 --> 00:39:04,380
It's a form of theft.

872
00:39:04,380 --> 00:39:07,380
If you take some of Letterman's 31 million,

873
00:39:07,380 --> 00:39:09,380
tax it away to support

874
00:39:09,380 --> 00:39:11,380
public schools

875
00:39:11,380 --> 00:39:13,380
against his will,

876
00:39:13,380 --> 00:39:16,380
the state is really doing no better

877
00:39:16,380 --> 00:39:19,380
than stealing from him.

878
00:39:19,380 --> 00:39:21,380
It's coercion.

879
00:39:21,380 --> 00:39:23,380
And the reason is,

880
00:39:23,380 --> 00:39:26,380
we have to think of ourselves

881
00:39:26,380 --> 00:39:29,380
as owning our talents and endowments.

882
00:39:29,380 --> 00:39:32,380
Because otherwise, we're back to just using people

883
00:39:32,380 --> 00:39:34,380
and coercing people.

884
00:39:34,380 --> 00:39:37,380
That's the libertarian reply.

885
00:39:37,380 --> 00:39:38,380
What's Rawls's answer?

886
00:39:38,380 --> 00:39:41,380
What's Rawls's answer to that objection?

887
00:39:47,380 --> 00:39:52,380
He doesn't address the idea of self-ownership directly.

888
00:39:52,380 --> 00:39:54,380
But the effect,

889
00:39:54,380 --> 00:39:56,380
the moral weight of his argument

890
00:39:56,380 --> 00:39:59,380
for the difference principle is,

891
00:39:59,380 --> 00:40:01,380
maybe we don't own ourselves

892
00:40:01,380 --> 00:40:04,380
in that thoroughgoing sense after all.

893
00:40:05,380 --> 00:40:07,380
Now, he says this doesn't mean

894
00:40:07,380 --> 00:40:09,380
that the state

895
00:40:09,380 --> 00:40:11,380
is an owner in me

896
00:40:11,380 --> 00:40:13,380
in the sense that it can simply

897
00:40:13,380 --> 00:40:16,380
commandeer my life.

898
00:40:16,380 --> 00:40:18,380
Because remember, the first principle

899
00:40:18,380 --> 00:40:22,380
we would agree to behind the veil of ignorance

900
00:40:22,380 --> 00:40:26,380
is the principle of equal basic liberties.

901
00:40:26,380 --> 00:40:28,380
Freedom of speech, religious liberty,

902
00:40:28,380 --> 00:40:32,380
freedom of conscience, and the like.

903
00:40:32,380 --> 00:40:35,380
So the only respect

904
00:40:35,380 --> 00:40:42,380
in which the idea of self-ownership must give way

905
00:40:42,380 --> 00:40:47,380
comes when we're thinking about whether

906
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:50,380
I own myself in the sense that

907
00:40:50,380 --> 00:40:54,380
I have a privileged claim

908
00:40:54,380 --> 00:40:56,380
on the benefits that come

909
00:40:56,380 --> 00:40:58,380
from the exercise of my talents

910
00:40:58,380 --> 00:41:00,380
in a market economy.

911
00:41:00,380 --> 00:41:03,380
And Rawls says, on reflection, we don't.

912
00:41:03,380 --> 00:41:05,380
We can define that as,

913
00:41:05,380 --> 00:41:07,380
we can defend rights,

914
00:41:07,380 --> 00:41:09,380
we can respect the individual,

915
00:41:09,380 --> 00:41:14,380
we can uphold human dignity

916
00:41:14,380 --> 00:41:19,380
without embracing the idea of self-possession.

917
00:41:22,380 --> 00:41:26,380
That, in effect, is his reply

918
00:41:26,380 --> 00:41:28,380
to the libertarian.

919
00:41:28,380 --> 00:41:30,380
I want to turn now

920
00:41:30,380 --> 00:41:32,380
to his reply to the defender

921
00:41:32,380 --> 00:41:34,380
of a meritocratic conception,

922
00:41:34,380 --> 00:41:36,380
who invokes effort

923
00:41:36,380 --> 00:41:39,380
as the basis of moral desert.

924
00:41:39,380 --> 00:41:46,380
People who work hard to develop their talents

925
00:41:46,380 --> 00:41:49,380
deserve the benefits

926
00:41:49,380 --> 00:41:52,380
that come from the exercise of their talents.

927
00:41:52,380 --> 00:41:54,380
Well, we've already seen

928
00:41:54,380 --> 00:41:57,380
the beginning of Rawls's answer

929
00:41:57,380 --> 00:41:59,380
to that question,

930
00:41:59,380 --> 00:42:01,380
and it goes back to that

931
00:42:01,380 --> 00:42:03,380
poll we took about birth order.

932
00:42:03,380 --> 00:42:07,380
His first answer is,

933
00:42:07,380 --> 00:42:10,380
even the work ethic,

934
00:42:10,380 --> 00:42:13,380
even the willingness to strive conscientiously,

935
00:42:13,380 --> 00:42:16,380
depends on all sorts of family circumstances

936
00:42:16,380 --> 00:42:18,380
and social and cultural contingencies

937
00:42:18,380 --> 00:42:21,380
for which we can claim no credit.

938
00:42:21,380 --> 00:42:23,380
You can't claim credit for the fact that

939
00:42:23,380 --> 00:42:26,380
you, most of you, most of us,

940
00:42:26,380 --> 00:42:28,380
happen to be first in birth order,

941
00:42:28,380 --> 00:42:32,380
and that for some complex psychological and social reasons,

942
00:42:32,380 --> 00:42:34,380
that seems to be associated

943
00:42:34,380 --> 00:42:38,380
with striving, with achieving, with effort.

944
00:42:38,380 --> 00:42:41,380
That's one answer.

945
00:42:41,380 --> 00:42:44,380
There's a second answer.

946
00:42:44,380 --> 00:42:49,380
Those of you who invoke effort,

947
00:42:49,380 --> 00:42:52,380
you don't really believe

948
00:42:52,380 --> 00:42:56,380
that moral desert attaches to effort.

949
00:42:56,380 --> 00:42:58,380
Take two construction workers.

950
00:42:58,380 --> 00:42:59,880
One is strong and can

951
00:42:59,880 --> 00:43:01,380
raise four walls in an hour.

952
00:43:01,380 --> 00:43:05,380
Without even breaking a sweat.

953
00:43:05,380 --> 00:43:09,380
And another construction worker is small and scrawny,

954
00:43:09,380 --> 00:43:13,380
and has to spend three days

955
00:43:13,380 --> 00:43:16,380
to do the same amount of work.

956
00:43:16,380 --> 00:43:18,380
No defender of meritocracy

957
00:43:18,380 --> 00:43:20,380
is going to look at the effort

958
00:43:20,380 --> 00:43:22,380
of that weak and scrawny construction worker

959
00:43:22,380 --> 00:43:26,380
and say, therefore, he deserves to make more.

960
00:43:26,380 --> 00:43:28,380
So it isn't really effort.

961
00:43:28,380 --> 00:43:30,380
This is the second reply.

962
00:43:30,380 --> 00:43:33,380
To the meritocratic claim.

963
00:43:33,380 --> 00:43:36,380
It isn't really effort

964
00:43:36,380 --> 00:43:38,380
that the defender of meritocracy

965
00:43:38,380 --> 00:43:43,380
believes is the moral basis

966
00:43:43,380 --> 00:43:45,380
of distributive shares.

967
00:43:45,380 --> 00:43:46,380
It's contribution.

968
00:43:46,380 --> 00:43:49,380
How much do you contribute?

969
00:43:49,380 --> 00:43:52,380
But contribution takes us straight back

970
00:43:52,380 --> 00:43:55,380
to our natural talents and abilities.

971
00:43:55,380 --> 00:43:57,380
Not just effort.

972
00:43:57,380 --> 00:43:59,380
And it's not our doing,

973
00:43:59,380 --> 00:44:02,380
how we came into the possession of those talents

974
00:44:02,380 --> 00:44:05,380
in the first place.

975
00:44:05,380 --> 00:44:08,380
All right, suppose you accepted these arguments.

976
00:44:08,380 --> 00:44:10,380
That effort isn't everything.

977
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:12,380
That contribution matters

978
00:44:12,380 --> 00:44:17,380
from the standpoint of the meritocratic conception.

979
00:44:17,380 --> 00:44:24,380
That effort even isn't our own doing.

980
00:44:24,380 --> 00:44:26,380
Does that mean...

981
00:44:26,380 --> 00:44:28,380
The objection continues.

982
00:44:28,380 --> 00:44:31,380
Does that mean that according to Rawls,

983
00:44:31,380 --> 00:44:34,380
moral desert has nothing to do

984
00:44:34,380 --> 00:44:39,380
with distributive justice?

985
00:44:39,380 --> 00:44:41,380
Well, yes.

986
00:44:41,380 --> 00:44:45,380
Distributive justice is not about moral desert.

987
00:44:45,380 --> 00:44:48,380
Now, here, Rawls introduces

988
00:44:48,380 --> 00:44:51,380
an important and a tricky distinction.

989
00:44:51,380 --> 00:44:54,380
It's between moral desert on the one hand

990
00:44:54,380 --> 00:44:58,380
and entitlements to legitimate expectations

991
00:44:58,380 --> 00:45:00,380
on the other.

992
00:45:00,380 --> 00:45:02,380
What is the difference between

993
00:45:02,380 --> 00:45:06,380
moral deserts and entitlements?

994
00:45:06,380 --> 00:45:08,380
Consider two different games.

995
00:45:08,380 --> 00:45:11,380
A game of chance and a game of skill.

996
00:45:11,380 --> 00:45:15,380
Take a game of pure chance.

997
00:45:15,380 --> 00:45:18,380
Say I play the Massachusetts State Lottery

998
00:45:18,380 --> 00:45:22,380
and my number comes up.

999
00:45:22,380 --> 00:45:27,380
I'm entitled to my winnings.

1000
00:45:27,380 --> 00:45:30,380
But even though I'm entitled to my winnings,

1001
00:45:30,380 --> 00:45:32,380
there's no sense in which,

1002
00:45:32,380 --> 00:45:35,380
because it's just a game of luck,

1003
00:45:35,380 --> 00:45:38,380
no sense in which I morally deserve

1004
00:45:38,380 --> 00:45:40,380
to win in the first place.

1005
00:45:40,380 --> 00:45:42,380
That's an entitlement.

1006
00:45:42,380 --> 00:45:44,380
Now, contrast the lottery

1007
00:45:44,380 --> 00:45:47,380
with a different kind of game,

1008
00:45:47,380 --> 00:45:50,380
a game of skill.

1009
00:45:50,380 --> 00:45:57,380
Now, imagine the Boston Red Sox

1010
00:45:57,380 --> 00:46:00,380
winning the World Series.

1011
00:46:00,380 --> 00:46:02,380
When they win,

1012
00:46:02,380 --> 00:46:05,380
they're entitled to the trophy,

1013
00:46:05,380 --> 00:46:08,380
but it can be always asked

1014
00:46:08,380 --> 00:46:10,380
of a game of skill,

1015
00:46:10,380 --> 00:46:13,380
did they deserve to win?

1016
00:46:13,380 --> 00:46:15,380
It's always possible in principle

1017
00:46:15,380 --> 00:46:18,380
to distinguish

1018
00:46:18,380 --> 00:46:20,380
what someone's entitled to

1019
00:46:20,380 --> 00:46:23,380
under the rules

1020
00:46:23,380 --> 00:46:25,380
and whether they deserve to win

1021
00:46:25,380 --> 00:46:26,380
in the first place.

1022
00:46:26,380 --> 00:46:28,380
That's an antecedent standard,

1023
00:46:28,380 --> 00:46:31,380
moral desert.

1024
00:46:31,380 --> 00:46:35,380
Now, Rawls says distributive justice

1025
00:46:35,380 --> 00:46:38,380
is not a matter of moral desert,

1026
00:46:38,380 --> 00:46:41,380
though it is a matter of entitlements

1027
00:46:41,380 --> 00:46:44,380
to legitimate expectations.

1028
00:46:44,380 --> 00:46:47,380
Here's where he explains it.

1029
00:46:47,380 --> 00:46:51,380
A just scheme answers

1030
00:46:51,380 --> 00:46:54,380
to what men are entitled to.

1031
00:46:54,380 --> 00:46:55,380
It satisfies

1032
00:46:55,380 --> 00:46:57,380
their legitimate expectations

1033
00:46:57,380 --> 00:47:01,380
as founded upon social institutions.

1034
00:47:01,380 --> 00:47:03,380
But what they are entitled to

1035
00:47:03,380 --> 00:47:05,380
is not proportional to,

1036
00:47:05,380 --> 00:47:06,380
nor dependent on,

1037
00:47:06,380 --> 00:47:09,380
their intrinsic worth.

1038
00:47:09,380 --> 00:47:11,380
The principles of justice

1039
00:47:11,380 --> 00:47:13,380
that regulate the basic structure

1040
00:47:13,380 --> 00:47:15,380
do not mention moral desert,

1041
00:47:15,380 --> 00:47:17,380
and there is no tendency

1042
00:47:17,380 --> 00:47:19,380
for distributive shares

1043
00:47:19,380 --> 00:47:22,380
to correspond to it.

1044
00:47:22,380 --> 00:47:25,380
Why does Rawls make this distinction?

1045
00:47:25,380 --> 00:47:28,380
What morally is at stake?

1046
00:47:28,380 --> 00:47:30,380
One thing morally at stake

1047
00:47:30,380 --> 00:47:32,380
is the whole question of effort

1048
00:47:32,380 --> 00:47:34,380
that we've already discussed.

1049
00:47:34,380 --> 00:47:37,380
But there's a second contingency,

1050
00:47:37,380 --> 00:47:40,380
a second source of moral arbitrariness

1051
00:47:40,380 --> 00:47:42,380
that goes beyond the question

1052
00:47:42,380 --> 00:47:44,380
of whether it's to my credit

1053
00:47:44,380 --> 00:47:45,380
that I have the talents

1054
00:47:45,380 --> 00:47:48,380
that enable me to get ahead.

1055
00:47:48,380 --> 00:47:51,380
And that has to do with the contingency

1056
00:47:51,380 --> 00:47:53,380
that I live in a society

1057
00:47:53,380 --> 00:47:55,380
that happens to be a society

1058
00:47:55,380 --> 00:47:58,380
that is able to prize my talents.

1059
00:48:00,380 --> 00:48:03,380
The fact that David Letterman

1060
00:48:03,380 --> 00:48:07,380
lives in a society that puts a great premium,

1061
00:48:07,380 --> 00:48:09,380
puts a great value

1062
00:48:09,380 --> 00:48:12,380
on a certain type of smirky joke,

1063
00:48:14,380 --> 00:48:16,380
that's not his doing.

1064
00:48:16,380 --> 00:48:17,380
He's lucky that he happens to live

1065
00:48:17,380 --> 00:48:19,380
in such a society.

1066
00:48:22,380 --> 00:48:24,380
But this is a second contingency.

1067
00:48:24,380 --> 00:48:27,380
This isn't something that we can claim credit for.

1068
00:48:27,380 --> 00:48:31,380
Even if I had sole unproblematic claim

1069
00:48:31,380 --> 00:48:34,380
to my talents and to my effort,

1070
00:48:34,380 --> 00:48:36,380
it would still be the case

1071
00:48:36,380 --> 00:48:37,380
that the benefits I get

1072
00:48:37,380 --> 00:48:39,380
from exercising those talents

1073
00:48:39,380 --> 00:48:42,380
depend on factors

1074
00:48:42,380 --> 00:48:46,380
that are arbitrary from a moral point of view.

1075
00:48:46,380 --> 00:48:49,380
What my talents will reap in a market economy,

1076
00:48:49,380 --> 00:48:51,380
what does that depend on?

1077
00:48:51,380 --> 00:48:53,380
What other people happen to want or like

1078
00:48:53,380 --> 00:48:54,380
in this society?

1079
00:48:54,380 --> 00:48:57,380
It depends on the law of supply and demand.

1080
00:48:57,380 --> 00:48:59,380
That's not my doing.

1081
00:48:59,380 --> 00:49:02,380
Certainly not the basis for moral desert.

1082
00:49:04,380 --> 00:49:07,380
What counts as contributing

1083
00:49:07,380 --> 00:49:10,380
depends on the qualities

1084
00:49:10,380 --> 00:49:13,380
that this or that society happens to prize.

1085
00:49:13,380 --> 00:49:16,380
Most of us are fortunate to possess,

1086
00:49:16,380 --> 00:49:19,380
in large measure, for whatever reason,

1087
00:49:19,380 --> 00:49:21,380
the qualities that our society

1088
00:49:21,380 --> 00:49:24,380
happens to prize.

1089
00:49:24,380 --> 00:49:27,380
The qualities that enable us

1090
00:49:27,380 --> 00:49:30,380
to provide what society wants.

1091
00:49:31,380 --> 00:49:33,380
In a capitalist society,

1092
00:49:33,380 --> 00:49:35,380
it helps to have entrepreneurial drive.

1093
00:49:35,380 --> 00:49:37,380
In a bureaucratic society,

1094
00:49:37,380 --> 00:49:39,380
it helps to get on easily and smoothly

1095
00:49:39,380 --> 00:49:41,380
with superiors.

1096
00:49:41,380 --> 00:49:44,380
In a mass democratic society,

1097
00:49:44,380 --> 00:49:47,380
it helps to look good on television

1098
00:49:47,380 --> 00:49:50,380
and to speak in short, superficial sound bites.

1099
00:49:51,380 --> 00:49:54,380
In a litigious society,

1100
00:49:54,380 --> 00:49:56,380
it helps to go to law school

1101
00:49:56,380 --> 00:49:58,380
and to have the talents

1102
00:49:58,380 --> 00:50:00,380
to do well on LSATs.

1103
00:50:00,380 --> 00:50:03,380
But none of this is our doing.

1104
00:50:03,380 --> 00:50:05,380
Suppose that we with our talents inhabited

1105
00:50:05,380 --> 00:50:08,380
not our society, technologically advanced,

1106
00:50:08,380 --> 00:50:10,380
highly litigious,

1107
00:50:10,380 --> 00:50:13,380
but a hunting society or a warrior society.

1108
00:50:13,380 --> 00:50:15,380
What would become of our talents then?

1109
00:50:15,380 --> 00:50:18,380
They wouldn't get us very far.

1110
00:50:18,380 --> 00:50:20,380
No doubt some of us would develop other talents.

1111
00:50:20,380 --> 00:50:23,380
Some of us would develop others.

1112
00:50:23,380 --> 00:50:26,380
But would we be less worthy?

1113
00:50:26,380 --> 00:50:29,380
Would we be less virtuous?

1114
00:50:29,380 --> 00:50:31,380
Would we be less meritorious

1115
00:50:31,380 --> 00:50:33,380
if we lived in that kind of society

1116
00:50:33,380 --> 00:50:35,380
rather than in ours?

1117
00:50:35,380 --> 00:50:38,380
Rawls' answer is no.

1118
00:50:38,380 --> 00:50:40,380
We might make less money,

1119
00:50:40,380 --> 00:50:43,380
and properly so.

1120
00:50:43,380 --> 00:50:46,380
But while we would be entitled to less,

1121
00:50:46,380 --> 00:50:49,380
we would be no less worthy,

1122
00:50:49,380 --> 00:50:52,380
no less deserving than we are now.

1123
00:50:52,380 --> 00:50:54,380
And here's the point.

1124
00:50:54,380 --> 00:50:57,380
The same could be said of those in our society

1125
00:50:57,380 --> 00:51:00,380
who happen to hold less prestigious positions,

1126
00:51:00,380 --> 00:51:03,380
who happen to have fewer of the talents

1127
00:51:03,380 --> 00:51:07,380
that our society happens to reward.

1128
00:51:07,380 --> 00:51:11,380
So here's the moral import of the distinction

1129
00:51:11,380 --> 00:51:14,380
between moral desert and entitlements

1130
00:51:14,380 --> 00:51:16,380
to legitimate expectations.

1131
00:51:16,380 --> 00:51:19,380
We are entitled to the benefits,

1132
00:51:19,380 --> 00:51:21,380
but the rules of the game promise

1133
00:51:21,380 --> 00:51:23,380
for the exercise of our talents.

1134
00:51:23,380 --> 00:51:26,380
But it's a mistake and a conceit

1135
00:51:26,380 --> 00:51:29,380
to suppose that we deserve in the first place

1136
00:51:29,380 --> 00:51:31,380
a society that values the qualities

1137
00:51:31,380 --> 00:51:35,380
we happen to have in abundance.

1138
00:51:35,380 --> 00:51:37,380
Now we've been talking here

1139
00:51:37,380 --> 00:51:40,380
about income and wealth.

1140
00:51:40,380 --> 00:51:44,380
What about opportunities and honors?

1141
00:51:44,380 --> 00:51:48,380
What about the distribution of access,

1142
00:51:48,380 --> 00:51:54,380
of seats in elite colleges and universities?

1143
00:51:54,380 --> 00:51:58,380
It's true.

1144
00:51:58,380 --> 00:52:02,380
All of you, most of you first born,

1145
00:52:02,380 --> 00:52:05,380
worked hard, strived,

1146
00:52:05,380 --> 00:52:12,380
developed your talents to get here.

1147
00:52:12,380 --> 00:52:15,380
But Rawls asks, in effect,

1148
00:52:15,380 --> 00:52:18,380
what is the moral status of your claim?

1149
00:52:18,380 --> 00:52:21,380
To the benefits that attach

1150
00:52:21,380 --> 00:52:24,380
to the opportunities you have.

1151
00:52:24,380 --> 00:52:31,380
Are seats in colleges and universities

1152
00:52:31,380 --> 00:52:34,380
a matter, a kind of reward,

1153
00:52:34,380 --> 00:52:37,380
an honor for those who deserve them

1154
00:52:37,380 --> 00:52:40,380
because they've worked so hard?

1155
00:52:40,380 --> 00:52:42,380
Or are those seats,

1156
00:52:42,380 --> 00:52:45,380
those opportunities and honors

1157
00:52:45,380 --> 00:52:48,380
entitlements to legitimate expectations,

1158
00:52:48,380 --> 00:52:55,380
that depend for their justification

1159
00:52:55,380 --> 00:53:01,380
on those of us who enjoy them,

1160
00:53:01,380 --> 00:53:04,380
doing so in a way

1161
00:53:04,380 --> 00:53:08,380
that works to the benefit

1162
00:53:08,380 --> 00:53:10,380
of those at the bottom of society?

1163
00:53:10,380 --> 00:53:12,380
That's the question

1164
00:53:12,380 --> 00:53:15,380
that Rawls's difference principle poses.

1165
00:53:15,380 --> 00:53:18,380
It's a question that can be asked

1166
00:53:18,380 --> 00:53:20,380
of the earnings of Michael Jordan

1167
00:53:20,380 --> 00:53:22,380
and David Letterman

1168
00:53:22,380 --> 00:53:24,380
and Judge Judy,

1169
00:53:24,380 --> 00:53:27,380
but it's also a question that can be asked

1170
00:53:27,380 --> 00:53:30,380
of opportunities

1171
00:53:30,380 --> 00:53:33,380
to go to

1172
00:53:33,380 --> 00:53:35,380
the top colleges and universities.

1173
00:53:35,380 --> 00:53:37,380
And that's a debate

1174
00:53:37,380 --> 00:53:39,380
that comes out

1175
00:53:39,380 --> 00:53:41,380
when we turn to the question of affirmative action

1176
00:53:41,380 --> 00:53:43,380
next time.

1177
00:53:48,380 --> 00:53:50,380
Thank you.

